      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) so that the lines with the given equations are perpendicular, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the slope of the first line:**\n\n   The equation of the first line is \\(y = 2x + 13\\).\n   The slope of this line is the coefficient of \\(x\\) in the equation, which is \\(2\\).\n\n2. **Find the slope of the second line:**\n\n   The equation of the second line is \\(6y + ax = 6\\).\n   To find the slope of this line, we can rewrite it in the slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope.\n\n   \\[\n   6y + ax = 6\n   \\]\n\n   Subtract \\(ax\\) from both sides:\n\n   \\[\n   6y = -ax + 6\n   \\]\n\n   Divide every term by 6:\n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n   \\]\n\n   From this equation, the slope of the second line is \\(-\\frac{a}{6}\\).\n\n3. **Determine the condition for perpendicularity:**\n\n   Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\).\n\n   Let \\(m_1\\) be the slope of the first line and \\(m_2\\) be the slope of the second line.\n\n   \\[\n   m_1 m_2 = -1\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the slopes we found:\n\n   \\[\n   (2) \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\(a\\):**\n\n   Simplify the equation:\n\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n   \\]\n\n   Multiply both sides by \\(-3\\):\n\n   \\[\n   a = 3\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
