      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) that makes the given lines perpendicular, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Rewrite the second equation in slope-intercept form**:\n   \n   The given second equation is:\n   \\[\n   6y + ax = 6.\n   \\]\n   To rewrite it in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), solve for \\(y\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   6y + ax &= 6, \\\\\n   -ax + 6y &= 6, \\\\\n   y &= \\frac{-ax + 6}{6},\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n   leading to:\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the slopes of the lines**:\n\n   The first equation provided is:\n   \\[\n   y = 2x + 13.\n   \\]\n   This is in the slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope.\n   So, the slope of the first line is:\n   \\[\n   m_1 = 2.\n   \\]\n\n   For the second equation:\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1,\n   \\]\n   we see that the slope of the second line is:\n   \\[\n   m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Define a condition for perpendicular lines**:\n\n   Two lines are considered perpendicular if the product of their slopes equals \\(-1\\). Thus:\n   \\[\n   m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1.\n   \\]\n   Substituting the slopes of the lines \\(m_1 = 2\\) and \\(m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}\\):\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\(a\\)**:\n\n   Calculate the product and solve for \\(a\\):\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{a}{3} = -1.\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(a\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   -\\frac{a}{3} &= -1, \\\\\n   a &= 3\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Validate the solution**:\n\n   Using our derived value for \\(a\\), we verify by plugging it back into the equations checking whether the resulting lines indeed have slopes that make the product angle 90 degrees.\n\n   The slopes are:\n   \\[\n   m_1 = 2, \\quad m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} = -\\frac{3}{6} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   The product 