      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) that makes the given lines perpendicular, we need to analyze their slopes. Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\).\n\nFirst, identify the slope of the line given by the equation \\(y = 2x + 13\\):\n\\[ y = 2x + 13 \\]\nThis line is in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope. For the line \\(y = 2x + 13\\), the slope \\(m_1\\) is:\n\\[ m_1 = 2 \\]\n\nNext, we need to rewrite the second equation \\(6y + ax = 6\\) in slope-intercept form. Start by isolating \\(y\\):\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\nSubtract \\(ax\\) from both sides:\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6 \\]\nDivide by 6:\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1 \\]\nThus, the slope \\(m_2\\) of the second line is:\n\\[ m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} \\]\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\\[ m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1 \\]\nSubstitute \\(m_1 = 2\\) and \\(m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}\\):\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1 \\]\nSimplify:\n\\[ \\frac{-2a}{6} = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{-a}{3} = -1 \\]\nMultiply both sides by \\(-3\\):\n\\[ a = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
